UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>A laptop and a tablet can both open documents, play videos, and browse the web, so choosing between them is not always easy. The right choice depends less on which device is newer or more popular and more on what you actually do with it every day. A student who takes notes in class, a designer who draws, and a traveller who wants something light in a bag all have different needs. This guide compares the two devices across the situations that matter most, so you can pick the one that fits your day instead of paying for features you will never use.</p><h2>Work and Study</h2><p>For most office work and study, a laptop is still the stronger choice. A physical keyboard makes long typing sessions faster and more comfortable, and a larger screen lets you keep a document open beside a browser tab or a spreadsheet. Most laptops also run full desktop software, which matters if your work depends on a particular program for spreadsheets, coding, accounting, or presentations. If you write essays, prepare reports, or manage files all day, a laptop will usually save you time.</p><p>A tablet can handle lighter work well. Reading, answering short emails, reviewing documents, and taking handwritten notes are all comfortable on a tablet, especially with a stylus. Many students use a tablet as a digital notebook, writing by hand and keeping every subject in one place. The limit appears when the work grows: editing a long document, working with several windows at once, or using software that only exists in a desktop version. A keyboard case helps a tablet, but it rarely matches the feel and speed of a laptop keyboard for hours of typing.</p><h2>Drawing and Creative Work</h2><p>For drawing, sketching, and handwriting, a tablet has a clear advantage. Drawing directly on the screen with a stylus feels natural, and many artists prefer it to drawing on a separate graphics pad while looking at a monitor. Note-taking apps let you mix handwriting, diagrams, and typed text on the same page. If your main creative work is illustration, sketching, or annotating PDFs, a tablet is often the better tool.</p><p>A laptop is better when creative work needs heavy software. Video editing, three-dimensional design, music production, and large photo editing projects usually need the processing power and storage of a laptop, along with desktop programs that are not available on a tablet. Some people use both: a tablet for sketching ideas and a laptop for finishing the work. If you can only buy one device and your creative work is mostly drawing, choose the tablet. If it is mostly editing and rendering, choose the laptop.</p><h2>Travel and Everyday Use</h2><p>A tablet wins on portability. It is lighter, thinner, and easier to hold on a bus, in a waiting room, or in bed. Battery life is often long enough for a full day of reading and video, and the device wakes instantly. For travel, a tablet is an excellent companion for maps, tickets, books, films, and light communication.</p><p>A laptop is heavier and needs more space, but it is a complete workstation you can carry. If you travel for work and need to produce documents, join video calls, and present slides, a laptop is the safer choice. Many travellers compromise with a light, slim laptop that is almost as portable as a tablet while keeping a full keyboard.</p><h2>How to Decide</h2><p>Ask yourself three questions before you buy. First, what will you do on the device for at least an hour every day? If the answer is typing, choose a laptop. If it is drawing or handwriting, choose a tablet. Second, does your essential software run on a tablet? Check before you buy, because a missing program is a deal-breaker. Third, where will you use it? A desk and a bag favour a laptop, a sofa, a classroom, and a plane seat favour a tablet.</p><p>Also think about cost over time. A tablet plus a keyboard case and a stylus can cost almost as much as a basic laptop, so add up the accessories before comparing prices. Check the storage size too: a device that fills up in six months will frustrate you whatever its other strengths. Finally, if you already own one of the two, the other may add more value than a newer version of the same thing. The best device is not the most powerful one on the shelf. It is the one that matches the work, study, and travel of your real life, and that you will still be glad to pick up a year from now.</p>' WHERE id = 16;
UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>Budgeting sounds complicated, but one simple idea makes it manageable: split your income into three parts before the month begins. The 50/30/20 rule divides your after-tax income into needs, wants, and savings. Fifty percent goes to needs, thirty percent to wants, and twenty percent to savings and extra debt payments. It is not a law and it will not fit every life perfectly, but it gives you a clear starting point, and a clear starting point is what most budgets are missing.</p><h2>What Counts as a Need</h2><p>Needs are the costs you must pay to live and work. Rent or housing, basic groceries, transport to work or study, utility bills, essential clothing, and minimum loan payments all belong here. Health costs and basic phone service are needs too. The test is simple: if skipping the cost would put your housing, health, or job at risk, it is a need. Needs are often the hardest part of a budget to change, because they are tied to where you live and how you travel, so do not blame yourself if this part is larger than fifty percent at first.</p><h2>What Counts as a Want</h2><p>Wants are the costs that make life enjoyable but are not essential. Eating out, streaming services, new clothes beyond the basics, holidays, hobbies, and upgrading a phone are wants. Wants are not bad spending. A budget with no room for enjoyment is a budget you will abandon. The point of the thirty percent is to give fun a limit instead of letting it quietly take over the whole month. When money is tight, wants are the first place to look, because cutting a want does not put your home or job at risk.</p><h2>Savings and Extra Debt Payments</h2><p>The twenty percent goes to your future: an emergency fund, savings for a goal, retirement contributions where they apply, and payments above the minimum on debts. Building even a small emergency fund matters, because it turns a broken appliance or a medical bill from a crisis into an inconvenience. If you have high-interest debt, many people put extra payments here ahead of other savings, because reducing that debt is itself a form of saving. The exact order depends on your situation, and there is no single right answer for everyone.</p><h2>A Worked Example</h2><p>The following is an illustrative example only, using round numbers to show how the split works. It is not a real person and not a recommendation for any particular income. Imagine a monthly after-tax income of 1,000 units of your local currency. Under the rule, 500 units go to needs, 300 units to wants, and 200 units to savings and extra debt payments. Rent might take 300 of the needs share, groceries 120, transport 50, and utilities and phone 30. Wants might include 100 for eating out and entertainment, 100 for hobbies and subscriptions, and 100 set aside for a small trip. Savings might be 120 into an emergency fund and 80 as an extra debt payment.</p><p>Notice how the example forces choices. If rent alone takes 600, the needs share is already over fifty percent, and something else must give: either wants shrink, savings shrink for a while, or housing costs change over time. That is the rule doing its job. It shows you the trade-off instead of letting it hide until the money runs out.</p><h2>When the Split Does Not Fit</h2><p>In many places, housing alone can take more than half of an income, and a strict fifty percent for needs is impossible. That does not mean the rule has failed. Use it as a target and a measuring tool. Try 60/25/15 for a while, or 70/20/10, and work toward a better balance as your income or costs change. A student sharing a home, a family with children, and a single person living alone will all have different splits, and that is normal. What matters is that savings get a share every month, even a small one, before wants take everything left over.</p><h2>How to Start This Month</h2><p>Start with last month, not with a guess. Look at your bank statements or receipts and sort every cost into needs, wants, or savings. Add up each group and compare it with the 50/30/20 target. Then pick one change only, such as moving a fixed amount to savings on payday, before spending starts. Automatic transfers help here: money you never see is money you cannot spend by accident. Review the split once a month, adjust the percentages to your real life, and keep going. A budget is not a punishment. It is a plan that lets you enjoy the present without borrowing from the future.</p><p><strong>Please note:</strong> this article is general education, not personal financial advice. Your situation, tax rules, and available products differ by country and provider, and any rates or figures you encounter will vary. For decisions about debt, tax, or long-term savings, consider speaking with a qualified adviser in your own country.</p>' WHERE id = 17;
UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>Saving money on a small income can feel impossible. After rent, food, and transport, there may be almost nothing left, and advice to save a large share of your pay can sound like it comes from another world. But saving does not have to start big. Small, automatic steps add up over months, and the habit matters more than the amount at the beginning. This guide is for tight budgets: practical moves that work when money is short and every unit counts.</p><h2>Start With a Tiny, Fixed Amount</h2><p>Pick an amount so small that you will barely notice it, and save it every time you get paid. It could be the price of one snack, one ride, or one small treat each week. The number matters less than the regularity. A small amount saved every payday builds two things at once: a growing balance and the habit of paying yourself first. Once the habit is steady, you can raise the amount slowly, a little at a time, without feeling a sudden squeeze.</p><p>Choose a round target for your first milestone, such as one week of basic expenses, and celebrate quietly when you reach it. Early wins keep you going. If a month is unusually hard, reduce the amount instead of stopping completely. A smaller saving still keeps the habit alive, while stopping often means never starting again.</p><h2>Make It Automatic</h2><p>Willpower is unreliable at the end of a long day, so remove the decision. If your bank allows it, set an automatic transfer that moves your chosen amount to a separate savings account on payday, before you have a chance to spend it. If automatic transfers are not available, move the money yourself within an hour of being paid, before any other spending. Keeping savings in a separate account helps too: money in your everyday account feels available, while money in a separate place feels set aside.</p><p>If your income arrives irregularly, save a small share of each payment as it comes in, rather than waiting for a payday that may not arrive on schedule. Even a small share of each payment is better than waiting for a perfect month.</p><h2>Find Money Without Feeling Deprived</h2><p>Look for one small, repeatable saving rather than a dramatic cut. Cook one extra meal at home each week instead of buying it. Carry water instead of buying a drink. Walk a short distance instead of paying for a ride. Share a subscription with family instead of paying alone. None of these alone will change your finances, but together they can free the small amount you save each payday.</p><p>Be honest about what you will actually keep doing. Cutting everything enjoyable at once usually fails within weeks. Keep one small pleasure that matters to you, and cut the spending you barely notice or enjoy. A budget you can live with for a year beats a strict plan you abandon in a month.</p><h2>Build an Emergency Cushion First</h2><p>Your first savings goal should be a small emergency cushion, enough to cover a minor surprise such as a repair, a medical cost, or a few days without work. This cushion is what stops a small problem from becoming a debt. Keep it somewhere you can reach it quickly in a real emergency, but not so easy to reach that everyday spending dips into it. Once the cushion exists, you can start saving toward other goals, such as education, tools for work, or a future move.</p><p>If you already have debt, the balance is delicate. Minimum payments must come first, because missed payments bring penalties and stress. After the minimums, split any extra between the emergency cushion and extra debt payments. Once the cushion is in place, extra money can go more firmly toward reducing debt.</p><h2>Track Spending for One Month</h2><p>You cannot fix what you cannot see. For one month, write down every cost, however small. Use a notebook or a simple note on your phone. At the end of the month, look for patterns: small daily costs that add up, subscriptions you forgot, or spending that happens when you are tired or bored. Most people find at least one leak they did not know about. Fix one leak, and move the saved amount into your automatic saving.</p><p>Review your progress monthly, not daily. Daily checks create worry, monthly checks show real progress. If your income grows, even slightly, raise your saving before your spending grows to match. That single habit, saving the raise before spending it, is one of the most powerful ways a small income slowly becomes a stronger one.</p><p><strong>Please note:</strong> this article is general education, not personal financial advice. Costs, interest rates, and available accounts vary by country and provider, and what works for one person may not suit another. For decisions about debt or long-term savings, consider speaking with a qualified adviser in your own country.</p>' WHERE id = 18;
UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>A good trip does not need a large budget. It needs a plan made before you leave, because most overspending happens from small unplanned decisions: a taxi instead of a bus, a meal in the most expensive street, a hotel booked at the last minute. With a clear plan for transport, stays, and food, you can enjoy a trip fully and still come home without regret. This guide walks through the planning step by step.</p><h2>Step One: Set the Total Budget First</h2><p>Before you look at destinations or hotels, decide the total amount you can spend without borrowing and without touching money needed for rent, bills, or essentials at home. Divide that total into four parts: transport, stay, food, and activities, plus a small reserve for surprises. Writing the numbers down before you book anything is the single most useful step in budget travel. If the numbers do not fit the trip you imagined, change the trip, not the budget: fewer days, a closer place, or a cheaper season.</p><p>Keep the reserve separate in your mind. It is not extra spending money, it is protection against a missed connection, a price change, or a small emergency. A trip without a reserve is a trip where one surprise can force you into debt.</p><h2>Step Two: Choose Transport Early</h2><p>Transport is often the biggest cost, and it is usually cheapest when booked early. Compare the real total cost of each option, not just the ticket price. A cheap flight far from the city may need an expensive transfer, and a slightly dearer train may arrive in the centre. Check baggage rules, travel time, and the cost of getting to and from stations or airports before you decide.</p><p>Once you arrive, use local transport. Buses, metro systems, and walking are far cheaper than taxis and ride-hailing for most trips, and they show you more of a place. Buy a travel pass if you will ride often. If you rent a vehicle, check fuel, insurance, and parking costs in advance, because those can double the price of the rental itself.</p><h2>Step Three: Pick a Stay That Fits the Plan</h2><p>Stay costs vary hugely by location, season, and day of the week. Staying a short walk from the centre is convenient but often the most expensive choice. A clean, well-reviewed place slightly further out, near a bus or metro stop, can save a large amount over several nights. Read recent reviews carefully, and check what is included: breakfast, Wi-Fi, and taxes can change the real price.</p><p>Consider the kitchen question. A place with a kitchen or a shared kitchen lets you cook some meals, which saves money and gives you a break from restaurants. If you travel with others, sharing a larger room or apartment often costs less per person than separate rooms. Book early when you can, and check the cancellation terms before paying, so a change of plan does not cost you the whole stay.</p><h2>Step Four: Plan Food Like a Local</h2><p>Food is where budgets quietly disappear. Eat where local people eat, away from the main tourist streets, and you will usually pay less for better food. Markets, bakeries, and small family restaurants are your friends. Eat a good breakfast, carry water and a snack, and save restaurant meals for one special meal a day instead of three.</p><p>If your stay has a kitchen, cook breakfast and pack a simple lunch. Try street food where it is fresh and busy, because a busy stall is a good sign. Set a daily food limit and track it roughly, not to punish yourself but to know where you stand. One expensive meal is fine when it is a choice, three a day by accident is how a budget breaks.</p><h2>Step Five: Choose Activities With Care</h2><p>List the places you truly want to see, and put the rest in a maybe list. Many cities offer free walking areas, parks, markets, viewpoints, and museums with free days. A city pass can save money if you will use it fully, but it wastes money if you only visit one or two included places. Check opening days and free hours before you go, because a closed attraction is a wasted journey.</p><p>Leave empty time in each day. A packed schedule forces taxis, rushed meals, and tired decisions, all of which cost money. Slow days are cheaper and more enjoyable. Finally, track spending each evening for five minutes: transport, stay, food, and activities. If one part is over, trim the next day gently instead of waiting until the money is gone. A budget trip is not a trip without pleasure. It is a trip where every cost was chosen, and the memories are not followed by a bill you regret.</p>' WHERE id = 19;
UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>The FS AI website offers twenty free converters and calculators that you can use today without installing anything or creating an account. They cover everyday questions: how much fuel a trip will use, what a monthly budget looks like, what a loan payment might be, how to change one unit into another, and how many days sit between two dates. This article introduces all twenty tools, grouped by what they help you do, so you can find the right one in seconds.</p><h2>Money Tools</h2><p>Start with the budget calculator, which splits an income into needs, wants, and savings using the simple 50/30/20 idea. It helps you see the shape of your month before you spend it. The savings goal calculator works the other way round: enter a goal and a monthly amount, and it shows how long reaching it may take. The loan calculator estimates monthly payments from a loan amount, a term, and an interest rate you enter, so you can compare different terms side by side. The interest calculator shows how interest grows on savings or on a loan over time, and the currency converter changes one currency amount into another using a rate you enter yourself. Use these tools for planning and learning, the figures depend on the numbers you enter, and real rates and terms vary by country and provider.</p><h2>Fuel and Travel Tools</h2><p>The fuel cost calculator is one of the most useful tools before a road trip. Enter the distance, your vehicle fuel use, and the fuel price, and it estimates the fuel cost for the journey. The trip budget planner adds stays, food, and activities to that transport cost, giving you a full trip estimate in one place. The distance converter changes kilometres into miles and back, which helps when you read a guide written in different units. Together these tools turn vague travel guesses into a written plan you can check before you leave.</p><h2>Units and Measurements</h2><p>Everyday life mixes units constantly, and the unit converters handle the most common changes. The length converter changes metres, centimetres, kilometres, inches, feet, and miles. The weight converter changes grams, kilograms, pounds, and ounces. The temperature converter changes Celsius and Fahrenheit, which matters for recipes, weather, and health. The volume converter changes litres, millilitres, cups, and gallons for cooking and fuel. Each converter works instantly: enter a number, choose the units, and the result appears. They are useful for study, cooking, shopping, and any task where a number arrives in unfamiliar units.</p><h2>Date and Time Tools</h2><p>The date calculator finds the number of days between two dates, which helps with planning leave, deadlines, and countdowns. The age calculator works out an age from a birth date. The work-hours calculator adds up hours across a week from start and finish times, useful for checking a roster or a timesheet. The time duration tool adds or subtracts hours and minutes, which is handy when you add up travel legs or study sessions. These small tools remove the mental arithmetic that often causes planning mistakes.</p><h2>Everyday Helpers</h2><p>The remaining tools cover daily questions. The percentage calculator finds a percentage of a number, a discount, or a change between two numbers. The tip calculator splits a bill and works out a tip per person. The BMI calculator gives a general body mass index from height and weight, as a rough guide only, not a medical judgement. The word counter counts words and characters in a piece of text. All twenty tools run in your browser, and none of them asks for personal details or stores what you enter. Try the one you need today, and keep the tools page bookmarked for the next time a number needs answering.</p>' WHERE id = 20;
